3cCG1fARF7E9DIs-HInWMM-oS3NZlYytCsrFtlru~n7leKGuC3RR6WfxKDgegOplPYO7O4YKsH4gyawCnMyIhE8ceBAo1FqoLUyCKvxWqbbtl1BSXoA__&amp;Key-Pair-Id=APKAJ535ZH3ZAIIOADHQ" loading="lazy" fetchpriority="low" decoding="async"><div class="c x0 y1 w2 h2"><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y2 ff1 fs0 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws1">37.<span class="_"> </span>An example of a cofactor is <span class="ff2 ws0">Copper</span>.</div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y3 ff1 fs0 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws1">38.<span class="_"> </span>DNA replicates during the <span class="ff2">S phase</span>.</div><div class="t m0 x1 h3 y2f ff1 fs0 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws1">Hereâ€™s a concise version with two to three important lines for each topic:</div><div class="t m0 x1 h4 y30 ff2 fs1 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws1">1. Nucleoside vs. Nucleotide:</div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y31 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff2 fs0 ls0 ws0">Nucleoside<span class="ff1 ws1">: Nitrogenous base + sugar (ribose or deoxyribose).</span></span></div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y32 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff2 fs0 ls0 ws0">Nucleotide<span class="ff1 ws1">: Nucleoside + phosphate group(s); building blocks of DNA/RNA.</span></span></div><div class="t m0 x1 h4 y33 ff2 fs1 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws1">2. Stages of Meiosis II:</div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y34 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff2 fs0 ls0 ws1">Prophase II<span class="ff1">: Chromosomes condense; spindle fibers form.</span></span></div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y35 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff2 fs0 ls0 ws1">Metaphase II<span class="ff1">: Chromosomes align at the equator.</span></span></div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y36 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff2 fs0 ls0 ws1">Anaphase II<span class="ff1">: Sister chromatids separate.</span></span></div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y37 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff2 fs0 ls0 ws1">Telophase II<span class="ff1">: Four haploid cells are formed.</span></span></div><div class="t m0 x1 h4 y38 ff2 fs1 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws1">3. Cellular Membrane Functions:</div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y39 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Protects the cell, controls growth direction, and stores carbohydrates.</span></div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y3a ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Maintains shape and regulates molecule entry/exit.</span></div><div class="t m0 x1 h4 y3b ff2 fs1 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws1">4. Transcription:</div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y3c ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Transcribes DNA into RNA using RNA polymerase.</span></div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y3d ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Occurs in the nucleus (eukaryotes) or cytoplasm (prokaryotes).</span></div><div class="t m0 x1 h4 y3e ff2 fs1 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws1">5. Gel Electrophoresis Dye Use:</div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y3f ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Prevents samples from floating out of wells.</span></div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y40 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Helps track DNA movement through the gel.</span></div><div class="t m0 x1 h4 y41 ff2 fs1 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws1">6. Physical Barriers for Enzymatic Activit<span class="_ _2"></span>y:</div></div></div><div class="pi" data-data="{&quot;ctm&quot;:[1.000000,0.000000,0.000000,1.000000,0.000000,0.000000]}"></div></div></div></div><div data-page-index="2"><div class="pf w0 h0"><div class="page-content" style="display: none;"><div class="pc pc3 w0 h0" style="display:block"><img class="bi x0 y0 w1 h1" alt="" src="https://doc-assets.studocu.com/7942405e250fd6d0cead5d955f0af659/html/bg3.png?Policy=eyJTdGF0ZW1lbnQiOlt7IlJlc291cmNlIjoiaHR0cHM6XC9cL2RvYy1hc3NldHMuc3R1ZG9jdS5jb21cLzc5NDI0MDVlMjUwZmQ2ZDBjZWFkNWQ5NTVmMGFmNjU5XC9odG1sXC8qIiwiQ29uZGl0aW9uIjp7IkRhdGVMZXNzVGhhbiI6eyJBV1M6RXBvY2hUaW1lIjoxNzYzOTc4MTc2fX19XX0_&amp;Signature=P6cmc3KBar-fb93mOQNKsVEA5WElN3WFCKYq1qpKX9rw1wiwuKFnrk~WG4YF~zNNDAdHF5AlWxTmPLghX7bEMd6DL1-S2pB~nx4DLnkH4UE3XNrgai4S40ac5n-036vLLLJ0DbXjUERBh22CBsDQDULb~HJkSez198yfgMtw3qyrXH4YuGWCUsAbn36SZLNTzMM~OOI0V7Ik6jRlX70vFpK2qpLDc1VR~pH3cCG1fARF7E9DIs-HInWMM-oS3NZlYytCsrFtlru~n7leKGuC3RR6WfxKDgegOplPYO7O4YKsH4gyawCnMyIhE8ceBAo1FqoLUyCKvxWqbbtl1BSXoA__&amp;Key-Pair-Id=APKAJ535ZH3ZAIIOADHQ" loading="lazy" fetchpriority="low" decoding="async"><div class="c x0 y1 w2 h2"><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y2 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Cell and organelle membranes restrict enzyme access.</span></div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y3 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Cytoskeleton and extracellular matrix hinder diffusion.</span></div><div class="t m0 x1 h4 y42 ff2 fs1 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws1">7. Cell Cycle:</div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y43 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Interphase: Growth (G1), DNA replication (S), and preparation (G2).</span></div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y44 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Mitotic phase: Mitosis divides chromosomes, cytokinesis divides the cell.</span></div><div class="t m0 x1 h4 y45 ff2 fs1 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws1">8. PCR (Polymerase Chain Reaction):</div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y46 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Amplifies specific DNA using denaturation, annealing, and extension.</span></div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y47 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Used for diagnostics, genetic testing, and forensic analysis.</span></div><div class="t m0 x1 h4 y48 ff2 fs1 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws1">9. Flagella and Cilia Functions:</div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y49 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff2 fs0 ls0 ws0">Flagella<span class="ff1 ws1">: Move the cell (e.g., sperm).</span></span></div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y4a ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff2 fs0 ls0 ws0">Cilia<span class="ff1 ws1">: Move substances over cell surfaces or aid cell movement.</span></span></div><div class="t m0 x1 h4 y4b ff2 fs1 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws1">10. Alkaptonuria:</div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y4c ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Genetic disorder causing homogentisic acid buildup.</span></div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y4d ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Leads to dark urine and joint/connective tissue issues.</span></div><div class="t m0 x1 h4 y4e ff2 fs1 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws1">11. Role of Proteins:</div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y4f ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Structural support, enzymatic catalysis, and transport.</span></div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y50 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Immune defense (antibodies) and regulation of gene expression.</span></div><div class="t m0 x1 h4 y51 ff2 fs1 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws1">12. Principle of Gel Electrophoresis:</div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y52 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Separates DNA, RNA, or proteins by size and charge.</span></div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y53 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Negatively charged molecules migrate towards the positive electrode.</span></div></div></div><div class="pi" data-data="{&quot;ctm&quot;:[1.000000,0.000000,0.000000,1.000000,0.000000,0.000000]}"></div></div></div></div><div data-page-index="3"><div class="pf w0 h0"><div class="page-content" style="display: none;"><div class="pc pc4 w0 h0" style="display:block"><img class="bi x0 y0 w1 h1" alt="" src="https://doc-assets.studocu.com/7942405e250fd6d0cead5d955f0af659/html/bg4.png?Policy=eyJTdGF0ZW1lbnQiOlt7IlJlc291cmNlIjoiaHR0cHM6XC9cL2RvYy1hc3NldHMuc3R1ZG9jdS5jb21cLzc5NDI0MDVlMjUwZmQ2ZDBjZWFkNWQ5NTVmMGFmNjU5XC9odG1sXC8qIiwiQ29uZGl0aW9uIjp7IkRhdGVMZXNzVGhhbiI6eyJBV1M6RXBvY2hUaW1lIjoxNzYzOTc4MTc2fX19XX0_&amp;Signature=P6cmc3KBar-fb93mOQNKsVEA5WElN3WFCKYq1qpKX9rw1wiwuKFnrk~WG4YF~zNNDAdHF5AlWxTmPLghX7bEMd6DL1-S2pB~nx4DLnkH4UE3XNrgai4S40ac5n-036vLLLJ0DbXjUERBh22CBsDQDULb~HJkSez198yfgMtw3qyrXH4YuGWCUsAbn36SZLNTzMM~OOI0V7Ik6jRlX70vFpK2qpLDc1VR~pH3cCG1fARF7E9DIs-HInWMM-oS3NZlYytCsrFtlru~n7leKGuC3RR6WfxKDgegOplPYO7O4YKsH4gyawCnMyIhE8ceBAo1FqoLUyCKvxWqbbtl1BSXoA__&amp;Key-Pair-Id=APKAJ535ZH3ZAIIOADHQ" loading="lazy" fetchpriority="low" decoding="async"><div class="c x0 y1 w2 h2"><div class="t m0 x1 h4 y54 ff2 fs1 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws1">13. Ribosome Sites:</div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y55 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff2 fs0 ls0 ws1">A site<span class="ff1">: Holds incoming tRNA.</span></span></div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y56 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff2 fs0 ls0 ws1">P site<span class="ff1">: Forms peptide bonds.</span></span></div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y57 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff2 fs0 ls0 ws1">E site<span class="ff1">: Releases tRNA.</span></span></div><div class="t m0 x1 h4 y58 ff2 fs1 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws1">14. Cellular Membrane Components:</div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y59 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Lipids, phospholipids, cholesterol, proteins, and carbohydrates.</span></div><div class="t m0 x1 h4 y5a ff2 fs1 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws1">15. Euchromatin vs. Heterochromat<span class="_ _2"></span>in:</div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y5b ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff2 fs0 ls0 ws0">Euchromatin<span class="ff1 ws1">: Gene-rich and transcriptionally active.</span></span></div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y5c ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff2 fs0 ls0 ws0">Heterochromatin<span class="ff1 ws1">: Condensed, gene-poor, transcriptionally inactive.</span></span></div><div class="t m0 x1 h4 y5d ff2 fs1 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws1">16. Golgi Apparatus Functions:</div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y5e ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Modifies and sorts proteins from the ER.</span></div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y5f ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Directs proteins to lysosomes, membranes, or secretion.</span></div><div class="t m0 x1 h4 y60 ff2 fs1 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws1">17. Importers, Symporters, and Antiporters:</div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y61 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff2 fs0 ls0 ws0">Importers<span class="ff1 ws1">: Move one molecule in one direction.</span></span></div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y62 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff2 fs0 ls0 ws0">Symporters<span class="ff1 ws1">: Move two molecules in the same direction.</span></span></div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y63 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff2 fs0 ls0 ws0">Antiporters<span class="ff1 ws1">: Move two molecules in opposite directions.</span></span></div><div class="t m0 x1 h4 y64 ff2 fs1 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws1">18. Nucleus:</div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y65 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Membrane-bound organelle containing DNA.</span></div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y66 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Controls cell growth, reproduction, and gene expression.</span></div><div class="t m0 x1 h4 y67 ff2 fs1 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws1">19. Amino Acid Structure:</div></div></div><div class="pi" data-data="{&quot;ctm&quot;:[1.000000,0.000000,0.000000,1.000000,0.000000,0.000000]}"></div></div></div></div><div data-page-index="4"><div class="pf w0 h0"><div class="page-content" style="display: none;"><div class="pc pc5 w0 h0" style="display:block"><img class="bi x0 y0 w1 h1" alt="" src="https://doc-assets.studocu.com/7942405e250fd6d0cead5d955f0af659/html/bg5.png?Policy=eyJTdGF0ZW1lbnQiOlt7IlJlc291cmNlIjoiaHR0cHM6XC9cL2RvYy1hc3NldHMuc3R1ZG9jdS5jb21cLzc5NDI0MDVlMjUwZmQ2ZDBjZWFkNWQ5NTVmMGFmNjU5XC9odG1sXC8qIiwiQ29uZGl0aW9uIjp7IkRhdGVMZXNzVGhhbiI6eyJBV1M6RXBvY2hUaW1lIjoxNzYzOTc4MTc2fX19XX0_&amp;Signature=P6cmc3KBar-fb93mOQNKsVEA5WElN3WFCKYq1qpKX9rw1wiwuKFnrk~WG4YF~zNNDAdHF5AlWxTmPLghX7bEMd6DL1-S2pB~nx4DLnkH4UE3XNrgai4S40ac5n-036vLLLJ0DbXjUERBh22CBsDQDULb~HJkSez198yfgMtw3qyrXH4YuGWCUsAbn36SZLNTzMM~OOI0V7Ik6jRlX70vFpK2qpLDc1VR~pH3cCG1fARF7E9DIs-HInWMM-oS3NZlYytCsrFtlru~n7leKGuC3RR6WfxKDgegOplPYO7O4YKsH4gyawCnMyIhE8ceBAo1FqoLUyCKvxWqbbtl1BSXoA__&amp;Key-Pair-Id=APKAJ535ZH3ZAIIOADHQ" loading="lazy" fetchpriority="low" decoding="async"><div class="c x0 y1 w2 h2"><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y2 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Central carbon, amino group (-NH2), carboxyl group (-COOH), hydrogen, and R group.</span></div><div class="t m0 x1 h4 y68 ff2 fs1 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws1">20. Applications of PCR:</div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y69 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Detects pathogens, mutations, and forensic DNA analysis.</span></div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y43 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Amplifies DNA for sequencing or genetic studies.</span></div><div class="t m0 x1 h4 y6a ff2 fs1 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws1">21. DNA Replication Methods:</div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y6b ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff2 fs0 ls0 ws0">Semi-conservative<span class="ff1 ws1">: Each new DNA has one old and one new strand.</span></span></div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y46 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff2 fs0 ls0 ws0">Conservative<span class="ff1 ws1">: Original DNA is preserved.</span></span></div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y47 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff2 fs0 ls0 ws0">Dispersive<span class="ff1 ws1">: DNA is mixed in new strands.</span></span></div><div class="t m0 x1 h4 y48 ff2 fs1 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws1">22. Plasmids:</div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y49 ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Circular DNA used for gene transfer (e.g., antibiotic resistance).</span></div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y4a ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Essential in genetic engineering and bacterial conjugation.</span></div><div class="t m0 x1 h4 y4b ff2 fs1 fc0 sc0 ls0 ws1">23. Lipid Bilayer Function:</div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y4c ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Structural component of membranes.</span></div><div class="t m0 x2 h3 y4d ff3 fs2 fc0 sc0 ls1">ï‚·<span class="ff1 fs0 ls0 ws1">Regulates diffusion and separates cell contents from the environment.</span></div></div></div><div class="pi" data-data="{&quot;ctm&quot;:[1.000000,0.000000,0.000000,1.000000,0.000000,0.000000]}"></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></article></div><div class="FloatingComponentsWrapper_floating-components-wrapper__ChJtH FloatingComponentsWrapper_align-center